\begin_layout Title
Econometrics I: Quiz 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a linear regression mdel written in a matrix form for 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 observations as 
\begin_inset Formula $y=X\beta+u$
\end_inset

, where the dimensions of the matrices are 
\begin_inset Formula $y:N\times1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $X:N\times K$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta:K\times1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u:N\times1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{t}$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is the estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
The least squares estimation (LSE) method finds 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 that minimizes the sum of squared residuals (SSR).
 Describe what the SSR is in words and then write it in equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
A residual is the difference between the observed value and the predicted
 value of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 If you square each residual and then add them together, you obtain the
 SSR.
 The SSR is the objective function of OLS estimation.
 We want to find 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 that minizes the SSR.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \min_{\beta}}\ SSR(\beta)={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}(y_{t}-X_{t}\beta)^{2}=(y-X\beta)^{\prime}(y-X\beta)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
One weakness of the LSE method is the outlier problem.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is it? Explain it in words (no graph).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Present an alternative estimation method that reduces the outlier problem
 and explain why it has less outlier effect
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
The outlier problem occurs when there are several data points that lie outside
 of the general area where the rest of the observations are contained, and
 thse points negatively affect the predicted values.
 An alternative estimation method that reduces the outlier problem is MAD
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{N}}\left|y_{t}-X_{t}\beta\right|
\]

\end_inset

 which minimizes the sum of the absolute value of residuals.
 It has less of an outlier effect because it doesn't punish points with
 large residuals as much as minimizing the SSR does.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Derive the first and second order conditions for the minimization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The objectie function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
{\displaystyle \min_{\hat{\beta}}}\ (y-X\hat{\beta})^{\prime}(y-X\hat{\beta})\\
{\displaystyle \min_{\beta}}\ y^{\prime}y-2\hat{\beta}^{\prime}X^{\prime}y+\hat{\beta}^{\prime}X^{\prime}X\hat{\beta}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

The first-order condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial SSR(\beta)}{\partial\hat{\beta}}=-2X^{\prime}y+2X^{\prime}X\hat{\beta}=0\iff X^{\prime}X\hat{\beta}=X^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset

and the second-order condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial SSR(\beta)}{\partial\hat{\beta}\partial\hat{\beta}^{\prime}}=X^{\prime}X>0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
What property does 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 have to satisfy for the second-order condition to be satisfied?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
For the second-order condition to be satisfied, 
\begin_inset Formula $N\geq K$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $rank(X)=K$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
If the condition in (4) is satisfied, what is the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

? Is it unique?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is the solution of the first-order condition 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\prime}X\hat{\beta}=X^{\prime}y$
\end_inset

.
 If the condition in (4) is satified, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y$
\end_inset

, and this solution is unique.
\end_layout

